<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jezebel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">Omride dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jezebel was a Phoenician princess, the daughter of Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians, who married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab of Israel</a> in the ninth century BCE. The Books of Kings portray her as a promoter of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and an enemy of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>, and tell how she was thrown from a window at Jezreel on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>'s orders after his coup. Her name became a byword for a wicked woman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jezebel.jpg" alt="Jezebel" width="320" height="587" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Byam Liston Shaw · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Liston_Byam_Shaw_003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jezebel is introduced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Book of Kings</a> as a Phoenician princess, the daughter of a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. The first Book of Kings, chapter 16, calls her Sidonian, a biblical term for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> in general. She married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, in the northern kingdom of Israel. Israel Finkelstein calls the marriage a brilliant stroke of international diplomacy that showed the power and prestige of Ahab and his kingdom. The name is an Anglicized form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> ʾIzeḇel, often interpreted as Where is the Lord?, a ritual cry in worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>. Ahab built a Baalist altar in a house of Baal in Samaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As queen, Jezebel according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> institutionalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> worship, killed Yahwist prophets and desecrated their altars. The prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> challenged the prophets of Baal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a> to a contest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a> to see which god would burn a bull sacrifice. Her prophets failed, and Elijah then ordered the people to seize and kill them. Jezebel vowed to kill Elijah, who fled to Mount Horeb. In the account of Naboth she resolved a failed deal between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> and Naboth over a vineyard by ordering the execution of Naboth and his sons on false charges of blasphemy. Elijah condemned this and foretold doom for her family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>'s death in battle their son Ahaziah inherited the throne, and his brother Jehoram succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> usurped the throne and killed Jehoram. When he came to Jezreel, Jezebel put on make-up and a wig and looked out of a window to taunt him. Geoffrey Bromiley reads this as the defiance of a queen mother. Jehu had her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> servants throw her from the window, and only her skull, feet and palms were left of her body, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> had prophesied.</p><p>Scholars disagree about the historicity of the story. Finkelstein considers it more of a historical novel than a chronicle, shaped by the theology of 7th-century BCE writers, while Brian R. Doak argues for its plausibility. A 9th-century BCE seal inscribed YZBL may once have read as belonging to Jezebel, but scholars do not agree. The name later came to stand for false prophets and then for fallen or promiscuous women.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jezebel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Close ties with the Phoenician cities, sealed by Ahab’s marriage to <b>Jezebel</b>, daughter of the king of Sidon, brought luxury goods and craftsmen, and the carved ivory panels found at Samaria show the court’s taste for Phoenician work.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible, which calls Sidon the mother city of Tyre and says the Sidonians long oppressed Israel, records Solomon's marriage alliance with the Sidonians and names <b>Jezebel</b> a daughter of King Ithobaal I of Sidon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century BCE Ithobaal I of Tyre, whom the Books of Kings call Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians, married his daughter <b>Jezebel</b> to Ahab, king of Israel, an alliance the biblical writers remembered with hostility.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Tel Jezreel, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-835" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jezebel"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5382 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5382 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-835">What was here in 835 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ahab"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ahab</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 9th century BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jehoram-of-israel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jehoram of Israel</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; r. c. 852–841 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/athaliah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/athaliah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Athaliah</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; d. c. 835 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Dost thou now govern the kingdom of Israel? arise, and eat bread, and let thine heart be merry: I will give thee the vineyard of Naboth the Jezreelite.&#8221;</p><footer>1 Kings 21:7, to her husband, when Naboth would not sell his vineyard &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as the First Book of Kings tells it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/1_Kings" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Had Zimri peace, who slew his master?&#8221;</p><footer>2 Kings 9:31, from her window, as Jehu came through the gate &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as the Second Book of Kings tells it; Zimri had killed his king and reigned seven days &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/2_Kings" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
